     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\":4,\"rationale\":\"The target crownData:exfiltrate is an OR of vaultRead, codeRepo, shareMount and dbReplica, so all four must be cut. One valid 4-step cut is shareMount, codeRepo, ciRunner and monitoringHost. Blocking shareMount cuts the workstation/mailClient/ciRunner -> kerberoast -> backupHost -> shareMount chain. Blocking codeRepo removes it directly. Blocking ciRunner stops both apiToken (so domainAdmin fails) and vpnSession (so serviceAccount fails), which kills vaultRead and dbReplica's privilege paths. Blocking monitoringHost removes dbReplica's last source (rdpLogin -> monitoringHost). Three blocks are not enough. One block is always needed for the shareMount chain, whose nodes lie on no other path to the target. That leaves two blocks for codeRepo, vaultRead and dbReplica. If codeRepo itself is blocked, one more node must stop both vaultRead and dbReplica. No such node exists, because the monitoringHost path (rdpLogin) and the domainAdmin/serviceAccount paths share no blockable common ancestor. If codeRepo is not blocked, its three sources need two blocks: one on the sshKey/jumpHost path and one at configSecret or containerHost to stop printServer. monitoringHost would then still reach dbReplica. So the minimum is 4.\"}"
    }
   ],
